From: Yury Klushin <digger@live.ru>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 14:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] pdp11: say which calls pass arguments in registers

The register convention reaches only a function the compiler can see
every call to, which leaves out everything declared in a header,
everything whose address is taken, every call through a pointer, and the
whole C library -- all of them the places where a call is cheap enough
for its arguments to be the cost.

Two ways in, for the two kinds of case.

A "regargs" attribute, and "noregargs" the other way, both on the
function's type rather than on its declaration.  On the type, because
then a pointer carries the convention with it and two types that
disagree are not compatible: assigning a regargs function to a plain
function pointer is a diagnostic, not a program that leaves its
arguments where nobody looks.  TARGET_COMP_TYPE_ATTRIBUTES is what makes
that so.  An indirect call through such a pointer passes in registers
too, which no amount of looking at the call site could ever establish.
A variable argument list refuses the attribute outright rather than
quietly ignoring it, since the declaration and the definition would
otherwise disagree about where the arguments are.

And a list of names in the back end for the library's memory and string
functions -- memcpy, memset, strlen and the rest of that set.  An
attribute in a header cannot do this job: the compiler calls those
functions itself, a structure assignment becoming a memcpy and a fill a
memset, and it does so from units that may never have declared them, so
half the calls would use one convention and half the other.  A name is
the same everywhere, including when the library's own definition is
compiled, which is what makes the two sides agree.  For that reason
neither the list nor the attribute answers to -mreg-args: a library is
built once, and a flag on one compilation must not be able to part it
from its callers.

What it is worth.  Three functions with the signatures of strlen, strcmp
and memcpy, called twenty thousand times each: 167 frames against 184,
so 45 ticks a call.  Against a run of the library's own strlen, memcpy,
strcmp and memset over eight to twelve bytes -- 770 frames for eighty
thousand calls -- that is about 3%, and more where the operands are
shorter, nothing where the compiler expands the operation inline
instead.  The attribute on a hot leaf function of one's own is worth a
good deal more: with two int arguments, 94 frames against 116.

The C library's convention is an ABI change: an object compiled before
this and linked against a library built after it disagree about memcpy
and its neighbours, with nothing to say so.  Both sides have to be
built from the same toolchain -- which, the library being shipped with
it, is what happens anyway.

Tested with four new target tests and by rebuilding libgcc and newlib
with it: the repository's own tests and examples pass, and a sample of
120 gcc.c-torture/execute tests -- the va-arg, structure-passing and
nested-function ones and the block beginning with 2 -- gives what it
gave before, 115 passes and five that do not build on this target.

---
 gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.cc                   |  166 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 gcc/doc/extend.texi                         |   32 +++++
 gcc/doc/invoke.texi                         |   10 +
 gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-4.c |   17 ++
 gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-5.c |   20 +++
 gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-6.c |   10 +
 gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-7.c |   22 +++
 7 files changed, 274 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff -urN a/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.cc b/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.cc
--- a/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.cc	2026-10-13 13:33:59
+++ b/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.cc	2026-10-13 13:33:34
@@ -219,6 +219,11 @@
 #undef  TARGET_MD_ASM_ADJUST
 #define TARGET_MD_ASM_ADJUST pdp11_md_asm_adjust
 
+#undef TARGET_ATTRIBUTE_TABLE
+#define TARGET_ATTRIBUTE_TABLE pdp11_attribute_table
+#undef TARGET_COMP_TYPE_ATTRIBUTES
+#define TARGET_COMP_TYPE_ATTRIBUTES pdp11_comp_type_attributes
+
 #undef TARGET_FUNCTION_ARG
 #define TARGET_FUNCTION_ARG pdp11_function_arg
 #undef TARGET_FUNCTION_ARG_ADVANCE
@@ -2363,6 +2368,147 @@
   emit_move_insn (mem, fnaddr);
 }
 
+/* The "regargs" and "noregargs" attributes say outright what
+   pdp11_arg_regs works out for itself otherwise, and they are the only
+   way to put the register convention on a call this unit cannot see
+   whole: a function from another unit, one whose address is taken, a
+   call through a pointer.  They sit on the function's type rather than
+   on its declaration, so that a pointer carries the convention with it
+   and a disagreement is a type error instead of a program that quietly
+   passes its arguments where nobody reads them.  */
+
+static const struct attribute_spec::exclusions attr_regargs_exclusions[] =
+{
+  /* Attribute name, function, type, variable.  */
+  { "noregargs", true, true, true },
+  { NULL, false, false, false }
+};
+
+static const struct attribute_spec::exclusions attr_noregargs_exclusions[] =
+{
+  { "regargs", true, true, true },
+  { NULL, false, false, false }
+};
+
+static tree
+pdp11_handle_regargs_attribute (tree *node, tree name, tree, int,
+				bool *no_add_attrs)
+{
+  tree type = *node;
+
+  if (TREE_CODE (type) != FUNCTION_TYPE
+      && TREE_CODE (type) != FIELD_DECL
+      && TREE_CODE (type) != TYPE_DECL)
+    {
+      warning (OPT_Wattributes, "%qE attribute only applies to functions",
+	       name);
+      *no_add_attrs = true;
+      return NULL_TREE;
+    }
+
+  /* A variable argument list is walked as one block of stack, so the
+     named arguments in front of it cannot start in registers.  Said out
+     loud rather than ignored: the declaration and the definition would
+     otherwise disagree about where the arguments are.  */
+  if (is_attribute_p ("regargs", name)
+      && TREE_CODE (type) == FUNCTION_TYPE
+      && stdarg_p (type))
+    {
+      error ("%qE attribute is not supported with a variable argument list",
+	     name);
+      *no_add_attrs = true;
+    }
+
+  return NULL_TREE;
+}
+
+TARGET_GNU_ATTRIBUTES (pdp11_attribute_table,
+{
+  /* { name, min_num_args, max_num_args, decl_req, type_req, fn_type_req,
+       affects_type_identity, handler, exclude } */
+  { "regargs",   0, 0, false, true, true, true,
+    pdp11_handle_regargs_attribute, attr_regargs_exclusions },
+  { "noregargs", 0, 0, false, true, true, true,
+    pdp11_handle_regargs_attribute, attr_noregargs_exclusions }
+});
+
+/* What a function type says about the register convention: the size of
+   the window, or -1 when it says nothing and pdp11_arg_regs is left to
+   work it out from the call itself.  */
+
+static int
+pdp11_declared_arg_regs (const_tree fntype)
+{
+  if (fntype == NULL_TREE)
+    return -1;
+  if (lookup_attribute ("noregargs", TYPE_ATTRIBUTES (fntype)))
+    return 0;
+  if (lookup_attribute ("regargs", TYPE_ATTRIBUTES (fntype)))
+    return PDP11_NARGREGS;
+  return -1;
+}
+
+/* Worker function for TARGET_COMP_TYPE_ATTRIBUTES.
+
+   Two function types are compatible only where they pass their
+   arguments the same way, so that a pointer to one cannot be used to
+   call the other.  */
+
+static int
+pdp11_comp_type_attributes (const_tree type1, const_tree type2)
+{
+  return pdp11_declared_arg_regs (type1) == pdp11_declared_arg_regs (type2);
+}
+
+/* The library functions whose argument convention is settled here
+   rather than worked out from the call.  Two reasons they cannot be
+   left to the attribute above.  The compiler calls some of them by
+   itself -- a structure assignment becomes memcpy, a fill becomes
+   memset -- and it does so from units that may never have seen a
+   declaration of them, so a convention carried by a header would not
+   reach those calls.  And a library is built once: the convention must
+   be the same wherever the name turns up, which is what a list of names
+   says and what an option cannot.  Both sides agree for the same
+   reason -- the definition in the library is recognised by its own name
+   when it is compiled.
+
+   The list is the hot memory and string set: arguments that are words
+   and pointers, and bodies short enough for the call itself to show in
+   a profile.  */
+
+static bool
+pdp11_regargs_libfunc_p (const_tree fndecl)
+{
+  static const char *const names[] = {
+    "memchr", "memcmp", "memcpy", "memmove", "memset",
+    "strcat", "strchr", "strcmp", "strcpy", "strlen",
+    "strncat", "strncmp", "strncpy", "strnlen", "strrchr"
+  };
+  tree id;
+  const char *name;
+  size_t i;
+
+  if (fndecl == NULL_TREE || TREE_CODE (fndecl) != FUNCTION_DECL)
+    return false;
+
+  id = DECL_NAME (fndecl);
+  if (id == NULL_TREE)
+    return false;
+
+  /* The name as written, and the same name behind __builtin_: a call
+     the compiler puts there itself arrives as the built-in function, and
+     it is the library's one that answers it.  */
+  name = IDENTIFIER_POINTER (id);
+  if (strncmp (name, "__builtin_", strlen ("__builtin_")) == 0)
+    name += strlen ("__builtin_");
+
+  for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE (names); i++)
+    if (strcmp (name, names[i]) == 0)
+      return true;
+
+  return false;
+}
+
 /* How many words of a call's arguments may be handed over in
    registers: PDP11_NARGREGS, or none at all.
 
@@ -2386,13 +2532,27 @@
 pdp11_arg_regs (const_tree fntype, const_tree fndecl)
 {
   cgraph_node *node;
+  int declared;
 
-  if (fndecl == NULL_TREE || TREE_CODE (fndecl) != FUNCTION_DECL)
-    return 0;
-
   /* A variable argument list is walked as one block of stack, so the
      named arguments in front of it cannot start in registers.  */
   if (fntype != NULL_TREE && stdarg_p (fntype))
+    return 0;
+
+  /* One of the library functions above: fixed, and for the same reason
+     not open to -mreg-args or to an attribute either.  */
+  if (pdp11_regargs_libfunc_p (fndecl))
+    return PDP11_NARGREGS;
+
+  /* Declared outright: that is the convention, whatever the call looks
+     like from here and whatever -mreg-args says.  A declaration and the
+     object built from it have to agree, and a flag on one of the two
+     compilations must not be able to part them.  */
+  declared = pdp11_declared_arg_regs (fntype);
+  if (declared >= 0)
+    return declared;
+
+  if (fndecl == NULL_TREE || TREE_CODE (fndecl) != FUNCTION_DECL)
     return 0;
 
   node = cgraph_node::get (fndecl);
diff -urN a/gcc/doc/extend.texi b/gcc/doc/extend.texi
--- a/gcc/doc/extend.texi	2026-10-13 13:33:34
+++ b/gcc/doc/extend.texi	2026-10-13 13:33:34
@@ -1697,6 +1697,7 @@
 * MSP430 Function Attributes::
 * NDS32 Function Attributes::
 * Nvidia PTX Function Attributes::
+* PDP-11 Function Attributes::
 * PowerPC Function Attributes::
 * RISC-V Function Attributes::
 * RL78 Function Attributes::
@@ -5402,6 +5403,37 @@
 By default functions are only callable only from other PTX functions.
 
 Kernel functions must have @code{void} return type.
+@end table
+
+@node PDP-11 Function Attributes
+@subsubsection PDP-11 Function Attributes
+
+These function attributes are supported by the PDP-11 back end:
+
+@table @code
+@cindex @code{regargs} function attribute, PDP-11
+@cindex @code{noregargs} function attribute, PDP-11
+@item regargs
+@itemx noregargs
+On this target the first two words of a call's arguments are passed in
+R0 and R1 where the compiler can see every call to the function -- see
+@option{-mreg-args} in @ref{PDP-11 Options} -- and on the stack
+otherwise.  These attributes say which convention to use outright, which
+is the only way to have the register one where the compiler cannot see
+the calls: a function from another translation unit, a function whose
+address is taken, or a call through a pointer.
+
+They belong to the function's type rather than to its declaration, so a
+pointer carries the convention with it and two types that disagree are
+not compatible -- assigning a @code{regargs} function to a plain
+function pointer is an error rather than a program that passes its
+arguments where nobody reads them.  For the same reason a declaration
+and the definition built from it must both carry the attribute.
+
+@code{regargs} cannot be used on a function with a variable argument
+list.  Neither attribute is affected by @option{-mno-reg-args}: a
+library and its header have to agree whatever flags either side is
+compiled with.
 @end table
 
 @node PowerPC Function Attributes
diff -urN a/gcc/doc/invoke.texi b/gcc/doc/invoke.texi
--- a/gcc/doc/invoke.texi	2026-10-13 13:33:34
+++ b/gcc/doc/invoke.texi	2026-10-13 13:33:34
@@ -30955,6 +30955,16 @@
 else keeps the standard convention, in which all arguments are pushed on
 the stack.  This is the default; @option{-mno-reg-args} passes every
 argument on the stack.
+
+Two things this option does not reach.  A function whose type carries
+the @code{regargs} or @code{noregargs} attribute (@pxref{PDP-11
+Function Attributes}) keeps what the attribute says, and so do the
+memory and string functions of the C library -- @code{memcpy},
+@code{memset}, @code{strlen} and the rest of that set -- which pass
+their first two words in registers always.  The compiler emits calls to
+those itself, from translation units that may never have declared them,
+so their convention cannot depend on what is visible or on how one side
+was compiled.
 @end table
 
 @node PowerPC Options
diff -urN a/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-4.c b/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-4.c
--- a/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-4.c	1970-01-01 03:00:00
+++ b/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-4.c	2026-10-13 13:33:34
@@ -0,0 +1,17 @@
+/* { dg-do compile { target pdp11-*-* } } */
+/* { dg-options "-O2" } */
+/* The attribute puts the register convention on a call the compiler
+   cannot see whole: a function from another unit.  */
+
+extern int att (int a, int b) __attribute__((regargs));
+extern int plain (int a, int b);
+
+int use (int x, int y)
+{
+  /* The attributed one takes its arguments in R0 and R1 ... */
+  /* { dg-final { scan-assembler "mov\t0?6\\(sp\\),r1" } } */
+  /* ... and the plain one has both of its pushed instead.  */
+  /* { dg-final { scan-assembler "jsr\tpc,_?plain" } } */
+  /* { dg-final { scan-assembler "mov\t0?6\\(sp\\),-\\(sp\\)" } } */
+  return att (x, y) + plain (x, y);
+}
diff -urN a/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-5.c b/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-5.c
--- a/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-5.c	1970-01-01 03:00:00
+++ b/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-5.c	2026-10-13 13:33:34
@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+/* { dg-do compile { target pdp11-*-* } } */
+/* { dg-options "-O2" } */
+/* The attribute is part of the type, so a pointer carries it and the
+   indirect call passes in registers too -- which the automatic rule can
+   never do, a function whose address is taken not being local.  */
+
+static int __attribute__((noinline,noclone,regargs)) taken (int a, int b)
+{
+  return a - b;
+}
+
+int (*volatile pf) (int, int) __attribute__((regargs)) = taken;
+
+int use (int x, int y)
+{
+  /* { dg-final { scan-assembler "sub\tr1,r0" } } */
+  /* { dg-final { scan-assembler "jsr\tpc,@_?pf" } } */
+  /* { dg-final { scan-assembler-not "mov\tr\[01\],-\\(sp\\)" } } */
+  return pf (x, y);
+}
diff -urN a/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-6.c b/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-6.c
--- a/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-6.c	1970-01-01 03:00:00
+++ b/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-6.c	2026-10-13 13:33:34
@@ -0,0 +1,10 @@
+/* { dg-do compile { target pdp11-*-* } } */
+/* { dg-options "-O2" } */
+/* Two types that pass their arguments differently are not compatible,
+   and a variable argument list cannot start in registers at all.  */
+
+extern int att (int a, int b) __attribute__((regargs));
+
+int (*p) (int, int) = att;   /* { dg-error "incompatible pointer type" } */
+
+extern int v (int n, ...) __attribute__((regargs)); /* { dg-error "variable argument list" } */
diff -urN a/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-7.c b/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-7.c
--- a/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-7.c	1970-01-01 03:00:00
+++ b/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/reg-args-7.c	2026-10-13 13:33:34
@@ -0,0 +1,22 @@
+/* { dg-do compile { target pdp11-*-* } } */
+/* { dg-options "-O2" } */
+/* The C library's memory and string functions pass their first two
+   words in registers wherever the name appears, with no attribute and
+   no declaration in sight: the compiler calls them itself, from units
+   that may never have declared them.  */
+
+void *fill (void *p, unsigned n)
+{
+  /* { dg-final { scan-assembler "clr\tr1" } } */
+  /* { dg-final { scan-assembler "jsr\tpc,_?memset" } } */
+  return __builtin_memset (p, 0, n);
+}
+
+unsigned len (const char *s)
+{
+  /* The only argument goes in R0, and nothing is pushed for it.  */
+  /* { dg-final { scan-assembler "mov\t0?2\\(sp\\),r0" } } */
+  /* { dg-final { scan-assembler "jsr\tpc,_?strlen" } } */
+  /* { dg-final { scan-assembler-not "mov\t0?2\\(sp\\),-\\(sp\\)" } } */
+  return __builtin_strlen (s);
+}
